Problem Description:
Consider the function f shown in the Karnaugh map below. d is don't-care, which means you may choose to output whatever value is convenient. Implement this function. 
//        x[1]x[2]
// x[3]x[4]   00 01 11 10
//  00 | d | 0 | d | d |
//  01 | 0 | d | 1 | 0 |
//  11 | 1 | 1 | d | d |
//  10 | 1 | 1 | 0 | d |

module top_module (
	input [4:1] x,
	output logic f
);

Erroneous Implementation:

always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
  case(x)
    4'h0: f <= 1'bx;
    4'h1: f <= 1'bx;
    4'h2: f <= 0;
    4'h3: f <= 1'bx;
    4'h4: f <= 1;
    4'h5: f <= 1'bx;
    4'h6: f <= 1;
    4'h7: f <= 0;
    4'h8: f <= 0;
    4'h9: f <= 0;
    4'ha: f <= 1'bx;
    4'hb: f <= 1;
    4'hc: f <= 1;
    4'hd: f <= 1'bx;
    4'he: f <= 1;
    4'hf: f <= 1'bx;
    default: f <= 1'bx;
  endcase
end

endmodule

Feedback:
m2014_q3.sv:137: error: Unable to bind wire/reg/memory `clk' in `tb.top_module1'
m2014_q3.sv:137: error: Failed to evaluate event expression 'posedge clk'.
2 error(s) during elaboration.

function.arguments:
{"code_completion": "module top_module (
	input [4:1] x,
	output logic f
);

  assign f = (x[3] & ~x[1]) | (x[4] & x[2]);

endmodule"}

content:
The error is that the implementation uses `always_ff @(posedge clk)`, but `clk` is not declared in `top_module`. Also, this function is purely combinational, so it should not be implemented as a sequential `always_ff` block.

A correct combinational implementation is:

```verilog
module top_module (
	input [4:1] x,
	output logic f
);

  assign f = (x[3] & ~x[1]) | (x[4] & x[2]);

endmodule
```
